# Adjacent design tokens

All UI and chart output must use Adjacent design tokens, never
hardcoded hex values or generic library colormaps (`viridis`, `tab10`,
`muted`, ...).

## Canvas and ink

- Canvas: `--comp-canvas` (`#ece9e2`)
- Paper: `#ffffff`
- Ink: `#0a0f0d`
- Deep: `#0e2a1f`

## Accent and series cycle

- Primary accent / up: `--comp-green` (`#3fae5a`)
- Down: `--comp-salmon` (`#e66b55`)
- Series cycle: `#3fae5a`, `#e66b55`, `#6fb7e0`, `#d89a3f`, `#a8c49a`,
  `#f0a8c8`

## Lines and grid

- Hairline: `#d6d2c8`
- Grid: `#ecebea` (dotted, behind data)

## Fonts

- Inter (titles/labels): `--font-main`
- Serif generic (deck): `--font-serif`. Not a named face.
- Monospace generic (ticks and data values): `--font-mono`. Not a
  named face. Inter is the only face the site loads.

Never name a font directly; use the token stacks.

## Chart shape

- Square corners (`--radius: 0`).
- Only the x baseline spine; horizontal gridlines only.
- A lone line is off-black `#0a0f0d`; color distinguishes series.
- Source credit bottom-left: `Source: Adjacent`. No wordmark, no
  divider rule, no basis clause.
- Axis ticks carry the unit on the top tick only; stated values use
  `0.00%`. Never `pp`.

## Python helper

Prefer `scripts/adjacent_chart_style.py` over re-deriving values by
hand. It carries the palette, the matplotlib/seaborn theme, the Plotly
template, and the source-line stamper.
